so welcome everybody to my talk about  the paper small hazard tree transducers  this is joint work of christophe lansin  moti medina and myself i am johannesbund  what we are studying is the complexity  of circuits in ternary logic  the variant of ternary logic we are  looking at was introduced by steven  colglini  what we do is we take the set of boolean  values and add a third logical value  the value is called u  which is an abbreviation for uncertain  you can be regarded as the superposition  of the boolean values which means that  you may evaluate  eventually to 0 1 or at some point  but the evaluation of u is  unpredictable so for worst case analysis  we say that uncertainty  propagates  a u will always stay a u  as an example for what is the difference  between binary and ternary logic we can  look at the law of non-contradiction  in binary logic x and not x will always  evaluate to zero for any value that we  plug into variable x  internal logic the law is not true  anymore we can plug in value u  then u and not u will evaluate to u  instead of zero  so  this is why the law does not hold  anymore  what is our motivation to look into  ternary logic well eventually the inputs  to a circuit will be analog signals  binary values are just an abstraction of  these analog signals with a third  logical value we cover analog behavior  that cannot be clearly interpreted as 0  1.  for circuits we want to compute the most  precise output in phase of uncertain  inputs  so  we call circuits that compute the most  precise output in phase of uncertainty  hazard free circuits  we can also define formally what is a  hazard free circuit  but to do so i need to introduce two  tools and map from the ternary world to  binary and back  there is the resolution that produces a  set of binary words from a ternary input  and a superposition that maps a set of  binary inputs to a ternary word  first the resolution is formally given  by the set of all binary words that  match input x in every position where x  is not uncertain  think of it as replacing each u  in x by  zero and one  second the superposition which is  denoted by the star operator  compares two binary words bit by bit and  replaces every position that does not  match by a u  the formal definition is given for two  words but because of associativity and  commutativity  this can be applied to any set  regardless of the order of application  take this example  for example the resolution of  0u  is the set 0 0 0 0 0 1 0 1 0 and 0 1 1.  so we replace every occurrence of a u by  0 and 1.  on the other hand the superposition of  words 0 1 0 and 0 0 1  is the word zero uu so  each position that does not match is  replaced by u  with these two tools at hand we can  define the hazard free extension of a  function  assume we are given a function f that is  defined in binary words  then its hazard freed  then its hazard free extension is  denoted by f subscript u  and it is defined by looking at each  possible resolution of the input  evaluating each resolution  and taking the star of all  of all evaluations  and that's basically the definition of a  hazard free circuit as well  a circuit c implementing function f has  a hazard if there is an input x  that may have some uncertainty  such that the output of c on x does not  match the hazard free extension of f  applied to x  so and finally a circuit is hazard free  if it has no hazards  please note that it is equivalent to say  that circuit c implements f subscript u  or  circuit c implements f and circuit c is  hazard 3.  before we come to our result let me show  you what is known on hazard free  circuits  first by huffman  we know that every boolean function has  a hazard free implementation  this means for any function f there is a  circuit c that implements f subscript u  next by ikenmeyer and his co-authors we  know that there is an exponential gap  between boolean circuit size  and hazard free circuit size  to be more specific there exists a  family of functions  such that for any function f from this  family the following holes  the smallest boolean circuit  implementing f  is  exponentially smaller than the smallest  circuit implementing f subscript u  so these two statements raise the  question whether there are functions  that have an efficient hazard free  implementation  finally this brings us to the results  that we want to present today  so  all transducers have an efficient hazard  free implementation  transducers are defined the usual way  there is a melee machine with state  space state space s  starting state s0  input alphabet sigma output alphabet  lambda and transition and output  functions tno  for an input sequence of fixed length n  the meaning machine induces a  transcription function tau  so the main result of the presented  paper is that there is a hazard free  circuit implementing transcription  function tau  and  the circuit has  size linear in n and depth logarithmic n  to be more specific the size is given by  2 to the size of the state space plus  the size of the input encoding times n  and the depth of the circuit is given by  the size of the state space times log n  plus the size of the input encoding  which means for constant size state  space and constant size input encoding  this is asymptotically optimal  though  constants might get large and  impractical for large machines  what is under the hood of this  construction is the parallel prefix  computation of lightning fissure  it is known for producing fast circuits  for addition and it is still used in  adders today  but there is more to it zartner and  fischer not only describe how to do  addition they actually present a  framework that can turn any transducer  into an efficient circuit  in general the problem is that states  traversed by the melee machine can only  be computed sequentially  the transition function t can only  compute state i  from the previous state as i minus 1 and  input sigma i  following this scheme would give us a  circuit of linear depth this would be  sub-optimal in their solution lartna and  fischer recognize that composition of  functions is associative  instead of applying the transition  function one by one on each new state  the transition functions are composed  together to function pi i  pi i then is applied to the starting  state s0  as function composition is associative  all pi i can be computed by a circuit  with logarithmic depth  unfortunately the scheme does not  directly apply to hazard free circuits  and the problem here is that encoding  matters a lot in the ternary world  for general construction lardner and  fisher hint at encoding the transition  function by a state transition matrix  in phase of uncertainty the state  transition matrix loses information that  cannot be recovered later  for example look at the depicted state  machine  if we are in state 0 and our input is  uncertain so it's 0 1 then we take  either transition 0 or we take a  transition 1. we cannot be sure whether  we end up in state 0 or state 1.  unfortunately the standard state  transition matrix loses track of the  fact that we are in either state 0 or  state 1.  in the example we can see that the state  machine certainly moves to state 0 if we  receive a clear zero input afterwards  but as the encoding loses strike we  cannot retain this information  our solution is to use a matrix that  encodes not only the transition from one  state to the next  but also the transitions for every  possible subset of the state space  this may seem redundant at first but it  is necessary to keep track of the  information that may be lost due to  uncertainty  getting back to the example if we get an  uncertain input the encoding will become  uncertain about state 0 and state 1  but remain but we remain a certain one  for the union of state 0 and 1.  and we can gain certainty about the  state for a subsequent input zero  so we don't lose  the certainty  before i come to an end i want to  present a possible application of this  framework this is ongoing research and  what we are aiming at is to apply this  framework to addition  a hazard free edition circuit can be  useful in the implementation of clocking  schemes for example  but here again  a major problem is the encoding  take for example the standard binary  encoding  and look at the encoding of 7 which  would be 0 1 1 1. and say we want to add  a single bit that happens to be  uncertain  then the hazard free extension tells us  that the most precise output we can  compute is the superposition of all  possible resolutions  hence we compute seven plus zero which  will be seven so encode it as zero one  one one  and we compute seven plus one which will  be eight so this is encoded as one zero  zero zero  the superposition of both results is the  word  uu  this output does not contain any  certainty at all and even though we know  the output should be seven or eight  hence  there is no chance in using the standard  binary encoding  in our solution we assume that the  uncertainty is bounded this is a fair  assumption for  the clocking applications we are aiming  at  please note that this assumption does  not help in the binary world at all the  previous example already shows that the  smallest amount of uncertainty leads to  fully uncertain output  so what we do is we define a new  encoding that is a combination of unary  encoding and gray code encoding  unary encoding alone would already  suffice to maintain the amount of  uncertainty in the inputs  but it has too much redundancy and is  very large  so please recall that the size of the  input encoding appears as an exponent in  the size of the circuit for our  construction  so we are looking for more efficient  encodings  but encoding still need to have enough  redundancy to cover the uncertainty of  the inputs  for a newly defined encoding addition  schemes are unknown so we have to design  according transducers  then finally we can apply the presented  work to obtain an efficient circuit that  is hazard free  so finally thank you for your attention
 

